  "evaluation": "Under the strict rules, `apparent` is limited to two specific cases: an underpowered null study (signaled in the abstract) or measuring fundamentally different things. Response A incorrectly uses `apparent` for an unstated direction and small sample size (when Study A was actually statistically significant, p = 0.006, not a null study). Responses B and C properly follow the rules by accepting the extracted directional conflict (`genuine`) while noting the unstated direction in Study A's abstract, and both correctly identify `population` (hospitalized COVID-19 patients vs. older CKD patients) and `outcome_measure` (PHQ-9 > 5 at 6 months vs. incident major depression at 1 year) as key differences. Response C is slightly more thorough than B by also capturing the difference in how vitamin D status was compared across participants (`comparator` / `intervention`).",
  "ranking": ["C", "B", "A"]
}
